<pdd-reason>Validate mathematical item and evidence metadata without claiming mathematical verification.</pdd-reason>
<pdd-version>0.1.0</pdd-version>

% Ownership and runtime
Own only src/lib.rs for the physics-contracts Rust crate. Preserve its typed public
Bundle/Definition/MathItem/Source/Locator/Evidence/Operation/Artifact/Checker/
Numerical/Resources types and enums, serialized field names, SCHEMA_VERSION,
MAX_INPUT_BYTES, MAX_RECORDS, ASSURANCE, valid_item_id, validate and validate_json.
Use Rust and pinned serde/serde_json; no CAS, provider, shell, network or foreign
runtime is part of metadata validation. Never edit independent tests or sources.

% Approved parent meaning
Implement F01 of the user-approved Rust mathematical physics platform plan.
Item IDs are source:location:namespace:label; location can be chNNN, frontmatter or backmatter. Srednicki chapters are 001..097;
namespaces equation/exercise/prose/diagram/example/definition/theorem are distinct.
Return typed validation reports with record_valid or invalid_input, not PASS or
proof claims. Successful validation MUST explicitly state metadata_validation_only
and that no mathematical proof, source certification, digest verification,
computation or artifact replay was performed. Declared digests are checked for
format only; source-review and outcome fields remain declarations.

% Required contracts
Use schema version mathphysics.contracts.v1. JSON rejects unknown/duplicate fields,
unknown enum values and missing required fields. Optional fields can be absent
or null. Each source has a nonempty path, lowercase 64-hex SHA-256, a one-based
PDF page or positive text-line range, and a declared source-review state.
Items require statement, explicit domain, sources, declared assumption/convention
references, separate implementation state and links to evidence IDs.
Evidence must refer to a real item with exactly the same statement, domain,
complete assumptions and convention. Links must be bidirectional and cannot
attach the same evidence to another item. Reject duplicated identities/references.
Require named operations with input/implementation digests, artifacts with
paths/digests, and strictly positive resource declarations. Duplicate artifact paths in a single evidence snapshot are invalid. Runtime budgets in
evidence are declarations, not OS resource enforcement. Never open source or
artifact paths as part of validation.

Numerical evidence uses approximate arithmetic with finite nonnegative tolerance
components (at least one positive), positive precision and an error explanation.
It cannot carry established as its outcome. Exact algebra, formal kernel and
certificate replay metadata use exact arithmetic and no numerical tolerance.
Formal/replay metadata require a versioned checker/digest referencing a declared
artifact digest. Other evidence kinds do not accept checker metadata. Approximate arithmetic always requires numerical error metadata, including external comparisons; non-approximate arithmetic cannot carry it. External
comparison and source derivation alone cannot claim established. Inconclusive,
unsupported and invalid-input attempts require a nonempty reason.

% Bounded behavior
JSON input is at most 1 MiB; total top-level and nested reference/source/artifact
records at most 10,000; per-record source/artifact/reference arrays at most 256;
text fields bounded to 16,384 bytes; identifiers bounded; errors capped at 128.
Use deterministic maps/sets and avoid quadratic cross-item backlink scans.
Malformed JSON and exhaustion produce structured invalid_input reports.

% Examples and evidence
srednicki:ch035:equation:35.1 and srednicki:ch035:exercise:35.1 are different IDs.
An undeclared assumption, mismatched statement or forged evidence link is invalid.
A well-formed fake formal-proof record may be record_valid, but this MUST NOT
assert the proof was checked. Preserve that assurance boundary everywhere.
Run cargo test --offline --locked and cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings;
independent tests include real CLI calls, bad references, overclaims, tampered
links, malformed input, nonfinite numerics through typed APIs and resource limits.
